%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 3fe2bd26d9376e04fbe4231cbdba4350, type: 3}
  m_Name: SparkAIConceptDatabase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ProjectSpark.AI.SparkAIConceptDatabase
  databaseId: project_spark_concepts
  concepts:
  - id: circuit_basics
    displayName: Circuit Basics
    description: An electrical circuit is a connected path that allows electrical
      energy to be transferred from a source through components and back to the source.
    learningGoal: Understand that an electrical circuit needs connected components
      and a complete path between the source terminals.
    playerUnderstanding: The player understands that a circuit is not just a collection
      of components. The components must form a connected electrical path for the
      circuit to operate.
    prerequisiteConceptIds: []
    recommendedLevel: 1
    requiredForProgression: 1
    teacherIntroduction: 'Every circuit starts with one simple idea: electricity
      needs a path to travel through the circuit. Let''s learn how the pieces fit
      together.'
    teacherExplanation: An electrical circuit is a connected path containing a source
      and other electrical components. For a basic circuit to operate, the path must
      connect from one source terminal through the circuit and back to the other
      source terminal.
    teacherExample: Think of a simple battery, wire, and lamp. When the wires connect
      the battery to the lamp and the path returns to the battery, the lamp can operate.
      If the path is broken, the circuit cannot operate normally.
    teacherQuestion: What must a circuit have before a connected electrical component
      can operate?
    teacherHint: Look at what connects the source to the other components. Is there
      a complete path?
    teacherStrongHint: The circuit needs a continuous electrical path from one terminal
      of the source, through the circuit, and back to the other source terminal.
    teacherSuccessMessage: Correct! A circuit needs a connected path for electrical
      energy to reach the components.
    teacherFailureMessage: Not quite. Remember that components must be connected
      together to form an electrical path.
    allowVoice: 1
    voiceEmotion: 1
    voiceSpeed: 1
    voicePitch: 0.5
    allowQuestion: 1
    allowHint: 1
    allowStrongHint: 1
    allowExplanation: 1
    allowExample: 1
    realWorldConnection: Every working electrical device contains one or more electrical
      circuits that provide paths for energy to reach the device's components.
    commonMisconception: Connecting components physically does not always mean that
      an electrical circuit is complete. Electrical terminals must be connected correctly
      to form a usable path.
    safetyNote: Learn circuit fundamentals using the low-voltage training equipment
      in Project Spark. Never experiment with mains electricity.
  - id: source
    displayName: Electrical Source
    description: An electrical source provides a potential difference that can drive
      current through a circuit.
    learningGoal: Understand that an electrical source provides the potential difference
      needed to drive current through a circuit.
    playerUnderstanding: The player can identify the source in a circuit and explain
      that the source provides the electrical potential difference needed for the
      circuit to operate.
    prerequisiteConceptIds: []
    recommendedLevel: 1
    requiredForProgression: 1
    teacherIntroduction: Before we can make a circuit work, we need something that
      provides electrical energy. This is called the electrical source.
    teacherExplanation: An electrical source provides a potential difference between
      its terminals. This potential difference can drive current when a suitable
      electrical path is connected.
    teacherExample: A battery is a simple electrical source. Its positive and negative
      terminals provide a potential difference that can drive current through a connected
      circuit.
    teacherQuestion: Which component provides the electrical potential difference
      in this circuit?
    teacherHint: The battery or power supply is the source. It provides the potential
      difference that can drive current.
    teacherStrongHint: The battery or power supply is the source. It provides the
      potential difference that can drive current.
    teacherSuccessMessage: Correct! You found the electrical source.
    teacherFailureMessage: Not quite. Look for the component that provides the voltage
      or potential difference for the circuit.
    allowVoice: 1
    voiceEmotion: 2
    voiceSpeed: 1
    voicePitch: 0.5
    allowQuestion: 1
    allowHint: 1
    allowStrongHint: 1
    allowExplanation: 1
    allowExample: 1
    realWorldConnection: Batteries, laboratory power supplies, solar cells, and generators
      can act as electrical sources in real circuits.
    commonMisconception: A source provides potential difference; it does not mean
      that current automatically flows when nothing is connected. Current depends
      on the complete circuit and its components.
    safetyNote: Use low-voltage training sources while learning. Never connect Project
      Spark circuits to mains electricity.
  - id: circuit_path
    displayName: Circuit Path
    description: A circuit path is the connected electrical route through which current
      can flow from one source terminal through the circuit and back to the other
      source terminal.
    learningGoal: Understand that current requires a continuous electrical path through
      the circuit.
    playerUnderstanding: The player can identify whether a circuit has a continuous
      path and understands that breaking the path prevents normal current flow.
    prerequisiteConceptIds: []
    recommendedLevel: 1
    requiredForProgression: 1
    teacherIntroduction: We found the source. Now we need to give electricity a path
      through the circuit and back to the source.
    teacherExplanation: For current to flow in a basic circuit, there must be a continuous
      electrical path from one terminal of the source, through the connected components,
      and back to the other terminal.
    teacherExample: Imagine a wire connecting a battery to a lamp, with another wire
      returning from the lamp to the battery. Together they create a complete path.
      If one wire is disconnected, the path is broken.
    teacherQuestion: Is there a complete electrical path from the source through
      the component and back to the source?
    teacherHint: Start at one source terminal and follow the connections. Can you
      reach the other source terminal?
    teacherStrongHint: Trace the entire circuit. The path must leave one source terminal,
      pass through the circuit, and return to the opposite source terminal without
      a break.
    teacherSuccessMessage: Excellent! You traced a complete electrical path.
    teacherFailureMessage: The path is incomplete. Start at the source and follow
      every connection until you reach the other source terminal.
    allowVoice: 1
    voiceEmotion: 4
    voiceSpeed: 0.95
    voicePitch: 0.5
    allowQuestion: 1
    allowHint: 1
    allowStrongHint: 1
    allowExplanation: 1
    allowExample: 1
    realWorldConnection: Electrical wiring in homes, machines, vehicles, computers,
      and electronic devices creates paths that connect power sources to electrical
      loads and other components.
    commonMisconception: Electricity does not simply travel from the positive terminal
      and stop at the component. In a basic closed circuit, current flows through
      a continuous path between the source terminals.
    safetyNote: Use the low-voltage Project Spark environment for circuit experiments.
      Never test circuit paths using mains electricity.
  - id: polarity
    displayName: Polarity
    description: Polarity identifies the positive and negative sides of a source
      or polarized component and helps determine how connections should be made.
    learningGoal: Understand that positive and negative terminals have different
      electrical polarity and that polarized components may require correct polarity.
    playerUnderstanding: The player can identify positive and negative terminals
      and make basic positive-to-positive and negative-to-negative connections.
    prerequisiteConceptIds: []
    recommendedLevel: 1
    requiredForProgression: 1
    teacherIntroduction: 'Our path is almost ready. There is one more thing to check:
      polarity. Positive and negative terminals must be understood before we connect
      them.'
    teacherExplanation: Polarity tells us which terminal is positive and which is
      negative. In a basic Project Spark circuit, the source positive terminal and
      negative terminal form opposite sides of the electrical supply. Polarized components
      can require their terminals to be connected with the correct polarity.
    teacherExample: If a battery has a positive terminal and a negative terminal,
      connect the circuit so the intended positive side reaches the component's positive
      or anode terminal and the return reaches the negative or cathode terminal when
      the component requires that polarity.
    teacherQuestion: Which source terminal should connect to the component's positive
      or anode side?
    teacherHint: Look at the symbols or labels on the terminals. Positive connects
      to the positive or anode side when the component requires that polarity.
    teacherStrongHint: 'Follow the polarity labels: source positive goes to the intended
      positive or anode terminal, and source negative returns to the intended negative
      or cathode terminal.'
    teacherSuccessMessage: Perfect! You matched the circuit polarity correctly.
    teacherFailureMessage: Check the polarity labels. The positive and negative sides
      are not interchangeable for polarized components.
    allowVoice: 1
    voiceEmotion: 5
    voiceSpeed: 0.95
    voicePitch: 0.5
    allowQuestion: 1
    allowHint: 1
    allowStrongHint: 1
    allowExplanation: 1
    allowExample: 1
    realWorldConnection: Polarity matters in batteries, LEDs, diodes, electrolytic
      capacitors, DC motors, and many other electronic components.
    commonMisconception: Positive and negative terminals are not simply two names
      for the same thing. Their electrical polarity can determine whether a polarized
      component operates correctly.
    safetyNote: Use low-voltage Project Spark circuits when learning polarity. Never
      experiment with mains polarity or mains wiring.
